## Dwelling caught a kernel-crashing DTB bug

While verifying the node-order fix was robust (not just lucky), I wrote a truncated-DTB test — and it immediately panicked. The `prop_name` helper indexed the strings block with an offset that came from *property data* (untrusted), so a corrupt or truncated DTB could push the index past the blob and panic — which on bare metal means the kernel crashes during device discovery. Real firmware can pass a damaged blob; QEMU's always-well-formed DTB never exercised this path.

Fixed `prop_name` to bounds-check (empty name on overflow), and audited every other slice in the walkers: the node-name slices are loop-bounded, and `be32`/`be64` and the reg-data reads all use `.get()` returning `Option`. The truncated-blob test now passes across all four walkers. I also confirmed the node-order fix is correct *for the right reason* on both arches: aarch64 lists slots ascending (lowest `0xa000000`), riscv64 descending (lowest `0x10001000`) — `device_reg_lowest` returns the true array start either way.

## CIBOSFS now runs on virtio-blk

The full filesystem stack works end-to-end on the virtio-blk device on both arches — the same arch-independent `Fs<D: BlockDevice>` layer x86 uses on ATA. The kernel formats CIBOSFS, creates `/etc`, writes a file, reads it back, then **remounts and re-reads** to prove the data persisted to the medium rather than RAM. Both aarch64 (slot 31) and riscv64 (slot 7) report "format/write/read-back/remount OK".

This is meaningful beyond the filesystem: it exercises the riscv64 Zicbom cache-maintenance path under real multi-sector I/O (not just one sector), and it validates the whole block→fs chain on real virtio-blk. No-disk degrades gracefully, x86 is unaffected, 383/0 tests.

