The i686 MMU "booted" last session, but scrutinizing it revealed the boot was passing only because of QEMU's leniency. PAE's top-level **PDPTE has a different format** from x86_64's PML4E — bits 1 (RW), 2 (US), and NX are *reserved and must be zero*. My first-cut encoder set `PRESENT|WRITABLE|USER` on every interior entry including the PDPT, which a strict CPU rejects when loading CR3. QEMU ignores reserved bits, so it "worked." This is exactly the QEMU-era shortcut you keep warning against.

