A serial console is a **byte stream with no inherent width**. The width lives in the *terminal* on the other end (minicom, PuTTY, a physical VT100, your monitor's terminal window) — not in the kernel or the UART. There's exactly one in-band way for the kernel to learn it: emit `ESC[6n`, and the terminal replies `ESC[rows;colsR`… **on the serial input line** — which we don't have wired yet. So true dynamic detection is genuinely blocked on the serial-input follow-on.

So: is the fixed 76 a **QEMU-era shortcut**? No — and it's worth being precise about why:

- **80 columns is the universal terminal standard** (VT100), and the default of essentially every terminal emulator and physical serial console. QEMU imposes *no* width — its serial goes to whatever terminal you attach. So 76 has nothing to do with QEMU; if anything it's aligned with *real hardware*, not QEMU.
