## The x86 DTB question — verified, and important

You pushed on the right thing. To be precise: **x86 has no DTB, and it shouldn't — but the behavior is still the same in principle.** Device Tree is the ARM/RISC-V convention; the PC platform (BIOS/UEFI and QEMU's x86) describes hardware via **ACPI + PCI + the E820 memory map**, never a DTB. So `dtb_ptr` is unused on x86 (`let _ = dtb_ptr;`).

The key point: the *principle* — "discover the variable hardware, hardcode only the architecturally-fixed" — holds on all four arches. On x86, CIBOS **does** discover the variable parts (PCI devices via config space + their BARs; memory via E820). It only hardcodes the **legacy ISA devices** (PIT `0x40`, PIC `0x20/0xA0`, COM1 `0x3F8`, VGA `0xB8000`) — and those are fixed on *every* PC, the x86 analogue of an architected register like `CNTFRQ_EL0`, not board-specific. Forcing a DTB onto x86 would be non-standard (nothing provides one). So x86 is aligned; no change needed. (If we ever wanted x86 to discover interrupt routing dynamically, that'd be ACPI MADT parsing — the ACPI equivalent of DTB — a real future option, but not needed since the legacy devices are genuinely fixed.)

## Ring buffer — why 128, and I raised it

128 was "generous headroom for a ~30-line boot." You're right that more is cheap insurance, so I **raised it to 256 lines × 160 bytes** (~41 KB static — trivial). 160 bytes means no logical line is truncated in the ring (longest is ~115). One honest limit: it's a *ring* (keeps the most recent N, evicts oldest). Capturing *truly* unbounded output can't be a fixed static buffer — that needs a heap-backed log (unavailable at earliest boot) or logging to disk. For boot review, 256 covers any realistic boot with large margin. Plain mode still keeps the full untruncated line as the authoritative capture.

## Scroll through errors/faults — added `[e]`

The review console now has **`[e] errors/faults only`** alongside `[r] replay full log` and `[c] continue` — the interactive counterpart of the boot-complete errors panel, so a paused boot can review just the faults ("— none —" when clean). I verified it. I did *not* add a line-by-line pager: `r`/`e` dump to the console and the terminal's own scrollback handles navigation — a real in-kernel pager is a possible nicety, not needed.

## USB export — not needed, and I agree

Your reasoning is exactly right. It'd require a full USB stack (host controller + mass-storage + filesystem writer) — a major subsystem — for a marginal gain, when the operator can already **view** the full retained log (more than Linux/Windows show at boot) and transcribe it. If on-disk logs are ever wanted, the natural target is the existing block/CIBOSFS path we already use for storage, not USB. So: fine without it.

